// GitHub issue #470: kernel/printk/decimal.tkb, checked where a test costs
// milliseconds.
//
// This exists because the thing it checks was wrong on one platform for as
// long as there was no such test. `uart_put_udec` was a copy per platform;
// QEMU's carried ten place values and RPi5's carried five, so on the board
// every number of six digits or more printed as its low five. 54000000 came
// out as `0` and 843750 as `43750`, and a truncated number reads exactly
// like a real one -- so the boot logs said the ARM generic timer's frequency
// register was returning zero, and then that it was returning a different
// value on every read, and neither was true. Two instrumented hardware boots
// and an investigation went into a timer that was working correctly.
//
// The values below are the ones that lied, plus the boundaries where the
// old five-place table changed behaviour, plus the ends of the range.
//
// Expected output (linux_user/decimal/decimal.expected): each value, once
// per line, printed by the kernel's own formatter.

fn show(value: usize) {
    uart_put_udec(value);
    uart_puts("\r\n");
}

fn app_main() {
    show(0);
    show(9);
    show(10);
    // The last value the five-place table could print, and the first it
    // could not.
    show(99999);
    show(100000);
    // What the board actually printed as `0`, and as `43750`.
    show(54000000);
    show(843750);
    show(1000000);
    // A tick count and a byte count of the sizes this kernel really logs.
    show(25532962);
    show(1073741824);
    // Both ends of the range the type can hold.
    show(9999999999);
    show(18446744073709551615);
}
